Dear Chris,=0A=0AYou missed my point.=0A=0AI've tested that code.=0A=0ASo, =
the seed itself is a long (first it is System.currentTimeMillis() - that me=
ans the most significant bits don't change because of its nature.)=0AThe le=
ft-shifts with 8 bits and the XOR would achieve to "update" all the bits of=
 the seed variable.=0AWith this code its impossible to update the 32 most s=
ignificant bits since the right side becomes an 32-bit int as you mentioned=
 as well. So the long update will be a 32-bit int!=0A=0AThat means even if =
there would be a 56-bit left shift (when i is 7 for example), because of th=
e 32-bit int that's a 24 bit left shift (the shift operation is circular) a=
nd the XOR will "update" the seed bits from 24 to 31.=0AThe bits from 32 to=
 63 of the seed are never changed by update! I think that's not the goal th=
ere!=0AThere should be a long cast before the shift operation.=0A=0A=0AAndr=
as=0A=0A=0A=0A________________________________=0A From: Christopher Schultz=
 <chris@christopherschultz.net>=0ATo: Tomcat Developers List <dev@tomcat.ap=
ache.org> =0ACc: Andras Rozsa <andras_rozsa@yahoo.com> =0ASent: Monday, Apr=
il 9, 2012 11:18 AM=0ASubject: Re: Tomcat 6  org.apache.catalina.session.Ma=
nagerBase issue=0A =0AAndras,=0A=0AOn 4/8/12 10:04 PM, Andras Rozsa wrote:=
=0A> Tomcat Developers,=0A> =0A> I am a UCCS student and the project I have=
 been working on is related=0A> to session ID generation.=0A> =0A> I have c=
hecked the source code of Tomcat 6 (6.0.24) and I think I=0A> have found a =
mistake.=0A>=0A> Line 567: long update =3D ((byte) entropy[i]) << ((i % 8) =
* 8);=0A=0AIn trunk (pre-6.0.36), the line of code is o.a.c.session.Manager=
Base:583.=0A=0A> This solution is not perfect.=0A> =0A> The update will be =
a 32-bit integer this way, so only the 32 LSB of=0A> the seed will be modif=
ied by entropy through the XOR. The byte=0A> casting should be replaced by =
a long casting.=0A> =0A> like this: long update =3D ((long) entropy[i]) << =
((i % 8) * 8);=0A=0AFor the record:=0A=0Aentropy is char[32]=0Ai is int=0A=
=0AHere is my analysis of the expression. All references are to JLS 7.=0A=
=0AFirst, we have "(byte) entropy[i]" which is a narrowing conversion (S=0A=
5.5) from char to byte, so we have only 8 bits of real data. On the=0Aother=
 side of the <<, we have all integer operations (32-bit), so we=0Aultimatel=
y have=0A=0Abyte << int=0A=0AThis represents a binary numeric promotion (S =
